# Property & Configuration File Standards

## Required Property Files
Every project must have at minimum:
- `config.properties` — unsecured properties common to **all** environments
- `config-{env}.properties` — unsecured properties per environment (local, dev, qa, uat, prod)

Secrets are managed via a secure vault (e.g., Azure Key Vault); `config-secure-{env}.properties` is not used.

## File Location
- All property files must be under `src/main/resources/properties/`
- Sub-folders by environment are acceptable for additional mapping files (e.g., `properties/dev/`)

## File Naming
- Use `.properties` format (not `.yaml` unless project-wide decision)
- Environment identifiers: `local`, `dev`, `qa`, `uat`, `prod`
- Pattern: `config-{env}.properties`

## Property Naming
- Property keys must be **lower case, dot-separated** with hyphens where needed
- Example: `common.api.client-id`, `http.port`, `usps.basePath`
- Group related properties with common prefixes

## Environment Loading
- Properties loaded via `config-${mule.env}.properties` pattern using `configuration-properties`
- The `mule.env` property determines which environment config to load

## Security
- **No clear-text passwords or secrets** in any properties file
- Sensitive values must be fetched from a secure vault (e.g., Azure Key Vault) or encrypted using AES CBC
- When a secure vault provider is used, declaring properties in `mule-artifact.json` `secureProperties` is **not required**

## Review Checklist
- [ ] All required config files exist for each environment
- [ ] No secrets or passwords in plain text
- [ ] Property keys follow lower-case dot-separated naming
- [ ] Files are in `src/main/resources/properties/`
